    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d417">

                <pb facs="0323" n="319" xml:id="pg319"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.512/1517: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Tientsin</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caldwell</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tientsin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-05-13T17:00:00+08:00">May 13, 1938—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 13—10:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your May 7, 10 a.m.<anchor n="10" xml:id="d417-f1"/> In view of the fact that my
                    colleagues, other than German and possibly Japanese, do not intend to comply
                    with the Chinese request regarding land tax as stated in my telegram of May 7, 3
                        p.m.<anchor n="11" xml:id="d417-f2"/> and confirmed to me again today, is it
                    still desired that Americans be advised to comply with request and pay tax under
                    protest?</p>
                <p>Sent to Embassy, Hankow, repeated to Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caldwell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d417-f1" n="10" xml:id="d417-f1-content">
                <p>This telegram from the Embassy in China repeated the Department’s telegram No.
                    141, May 5, 4 p.m., <ref target="#pg313">p. 313</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d417-f2" n="11" xml:id="d417-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
